{
  "Routes": [
    {
      "_comment": "Register/Login must stay anonymous at the gateway — there's no token to present yet.",
      "DownstreamPathTemplate": "/api/v1/Auth/register",
      "DownstreamScheme": "http",
      "DownstreamHostAndPorts": [{ "Host": "localhost", "Port": 5218 }],
      "UpstreamPathTemplate": "/Identity/Auth/register",
      "UpstreamHttpMethod": [ "POST" ]
    },
    {
      "_comment": "Brute-force protection on this route is applied in Program.cs via ASP.NET Core's own RateLimiter, partitioned by client IP — NOT via Ocelot's RateLimitOptions. Ocelot's rate limiter identifies a 'client' by a self-declared request header, which an attacker mounting a credential-stuffing attack would simply omit or vary; it's meant for per-tenant/API-key throttling, not abuse protection. See the A3 writeup for how this was actually discovered (it silently failed with 503s in testing).",
      "DownstreamPathTemplate": "/api/v1/Auth/login",
      "DownstreamScheme": "http",
      "DownstreamHostAndPorts": [{ "Host": "localhost", "Port": 5218 }],
      "UpstreamPathTemplate": "/Identity/Auth/login",
      "UpstreamHttpMethod": [ "POST" ]
    },
    {
      "_comment": "AuthenticationOptions is what makes Ocelot itself validate the JWT before proxying — an invalid/missing/expired token never reaches Identity.API at all.",
      "DownstreamPathTemplate": "/api/v1/Auth/me",
